# Domain libraries

- Pure functions. No I/O. No side effects while a module loads, other than building indexes and data-integrity guards (D-05, D-18).
- `plate-domain` depends only on `plate-data`. No other runtime dependency (D-05).
- Do not hardcode plate codes. Code tables are derived from `plate-data` (D-38). The § 30 ust. 1 alphabet and the five § 25 plate types stay in `plate-domain`; they are not registry fields (D-07).
- JSDoc on a public export states the contract: inputs, result variants, invariants, and every rule from `docs/decisions.md` that decides which variant or entry a caller gets — a reader of the JSDoc alone must be able to predict the result (D-45). A legal citation is the provision plus its row ID from `docs/pl-plate-rules.md` (D-33, D-41).
- Before asserting what `parsePlate` or `candidatesFor` returns for an input, list every reading of it: the column-4 letter with a reduced or diplomatic vehicle part, column-6 codes, every full code that is a prefix of the input or that the input is a prefix of, and look-alike swaps (D-32). The L3 entry appears while typing only when none of these can still be completed (D-39).
- `libs/plate-data/src/lib/pl/registry.ts` is generated by `libs/plate-data/scripts/import-annex-13.mts`; change the generator and regenerate, never edit the output by hand.
